resolution(...,Strategy=>...)
*****************************

Description
===========

Strategy => n -- an option for *note resolution: resolution, which specifies
which algorithm to use.  Strategies are specified by number and the algorithms
available are
  * Strategy => 0 -- Compute syzygies on the Gröbner bases of each syzygy
    module.  The algorithm uses important speedups due to R. La Scala. This
    algorithm appears to be on the average the fastest.
  * Strategy => 1 -- An older version of algorithm 0, which doesn't allow as
    much experimentation, but can sometimes be marginally faster.
  * Strategy => 2 -- Compute syzygies on the minimal generators of each matrix
    in the resolution.  Over quotient rings, it's preferred.
  * Strategy => 3 -- Same as algorithm 2, but compute those Hilbert functions
    which allow removal of S-pairs (a la Robbiano, et al.). Sometimes this
    improvement can be very dramatic.
All algorithms use induced monomial orders (Schreyer orders), since this makes
an enormous improvement to the efficiency of the algorithm.

Functions with optional argument named Strategy:
================================================

  * "addHook(...,Strategy=>...)" -- see *note addHook: (Macaulay2Doc)addHook,
    -- add a hook function to an object for later processing
  * annihilator(...,Strategy=>...) (missing documentation)
  * "basis(...,Strategy=>...)" -- see *note basis: (Macaulay2Doc)basis, --
    basis or generating set of all or part of a ring, ideal or module
  * "mingens(...,Strategy=>...)" -- see *note Complement:
    (Macaulay2Doc)Complement, -- a Strategy option value
  * "trim(...,Strategy=>...)" -- see *note Complement:
    (Macaulay2Doc)Complement, -- a Strategy option value
